# OS files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# The generated Tree-sitter parser is not ignored, on purpose. Six files under
# crates/cypcb-parser/grammar/src/ are committed - `parser.c` and the two JSON
# tables `tree-sitter generate` writes, plus the headers it vendors - because
# `build.rs` compiles them and refuses to build without them. Ignoring the
# directory they live in made `git add` demand -f, which is how a regenerated
# parser can be committed one file short, and hid any new file the generator
# started writing.
crates/cypcb-parser/grammar/node_modules/
# grammar/package-lock.json is tracked: it pins the tree-sitter CLI that
# generates parser.c, and the gate regenerates the parser and compares it
# against what is committed. Without the lockfile that comparison would be
# between two different generators.

# Environment files
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Local cargo config (machine-specific)
.cargo/config.toml

# FreeRouting JARs (download separately)
freerouting*.jar

# Runtime / generated artifacts
# `tauri::generate_context!` writes the ACL schemas here on every build of
# cypcb-desktop. They are derived from tauri.conf.json and the capabilities
# directory, both of which are tracked, so committing them would be committing
# a build output that changes whenever the toolchain does.
/src-tauri/gen/
logs/
output/
export-test/
.bg-shell/
.artifacts/

